# Everything else: see .env.advanced.example — you almost certainly don't need it.
#
# Qwen3.8-Flash-Next (W4A16) on two CMP 170HX cards. ./start.sh copies this file to .env
# on first run and reads only .env. Every setting is commented out and shows its
# default; uncomment a line to change it, then run ./start.sh restart.
# One KEY=value per line; a value on the command line beats .env for that run:
#   CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=2,3 ./start.sh restart
# The two cards to serve on, as nvidia-smi numbers them (exactly two, distinct).
# CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0,1
# Listen address: 127.0.0.1 = this machine only; 0.0.0.0 = every interface (set API_KEY first).
# HOST=127.0.0.1
# API port.
# PORT=8000
# When set, every /v1 request must send "Authorization: Bearer <key>"; unset = no key.
# API_KEY=
# The model id clients send.
# SERVED_MODEL_NAME=qwen3.8-flash-next
# Where the checkpoint goes, as an absolute path. Default: models/ in this repo.
# The disk only matters while loading: the n-gram table is copied into host RAM.
# MODELS_DIR=/path/to/models
# KV offload: GiB of host RAM that keeps KV blocks evicted from the cards, so a
# long shared context pushed out by other traffic comes back in under a second
# instead of being re-read (0 = off). 64 held ~2.2 M tokens in our runs.
# KV_OFFLOAD_GB=0
# A disk tier behind the RAM tier (a host directory; needs KV_OFFLOAD_GB > 0).
# Write-through and never pruned: give it a volume of its own.
# KV_OFFLOAD_DIR=
# Peer access: auto verifies content before enabling P2P.
# off disables it; force bypasses verification.
# P2P=auto
